  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "The function $C(t)=6{e}^{-2t}$ gives the concentration, in milligrams per liter, of a medication in the bloodstream $t$ hours after administration. Find the instantaneous rate of change of the concentration as a function of $t.$\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change of the concentration is\n{{field-1}}\nmilligrams per liter per hour.",
  "worked_solution": "The instantaneous rate of change of the concentration is equal to ${C}^{\u2032}(t).$\n\nNow, the rate of change of $C(t)$ with respect to $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{\u2032}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{e}^{-2t}) \\\\  & =6(-2){e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =-12{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change of the concentration is $-12{e}^{-2t}$ milligrams per liter per hour.",
